    <DataNode elementId="Lactosyl-Ceramides" textLabel="a lactosylceramide" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="17950" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>|FRAME: Glycosphingolipids Glycosphingolipids| are a family of complex lipids composed of |FRAME: Ceramides| and mono- or oligosaccharide moieties that play an important role in various cellular functions including recognition, cell adhesion, proliferation and differentiation |CITS: [8490246]|. Glycosphingolipids are synthesized by the sequential addition of sugar residues to ceramides by glycosyltransferases that are specific to each glycosidic linkage. |FRAME: Lactosyl-Ceramides Lactosylceramide|, a product of the second glycosylation step in glycosphingolipid biosynthesis, is an important branching point, being a common precursor for the synthesis of the three major classes of glycosphingolipids: the ganglio series, the lacto (and neolacto) series, and the globo series |CITS: [10320813]|. The fatty acid composition of |FRAME: Lactosyl-Ceramides lactosylceramide| is variable, but it comprises primarily |FRAME: PALMITATE|, |FRAME: DOCOSANOATE|, and |FRAME: TETRACOSANOATE|.</Comment>
